Take a look at nethack-el: http://www.nongnu.org/nethack-el/
It patches NetHack to output lisp-like code, and then emacs
talks to the NetHack process via pipes or something like that.

There's also NetHack Proxy ( http://www.nongnu.org/nhproxy/ ) which makes
NetHack output simple code that can be easily parsed by a special
program on the user's computer.



Both of those basically create a new windowport for NetHack, there's
documentation how to do that in the NetHack source tree in doc/window.doc
(which is a text file, not a word document, despite the extension)


